Problem: the Linux E2E CI suite intermittently failed capture_timeout_remains_bounded_when_writer_escapes_process_group because the test gave a newly spawned Rust test process only 100 ms to start, spawn its descendant, call setsid, and write a readiness marker while thousands of tests ran concurrently. The production capture timeout itself remained bounded. Objective: preserve both security guarantees without a scheduler race. Decision: split the mixed scenario into one dedicated 100 ms timeout-bound test using a slow helper, and one escaped-writer test whose helper gets a five-second startup ceiling and signals readiness after setsid; the latter still proves the inherited stdout cannot hold capture open. Production code and its eight-second capture budget are unchanged. Validation: cargo fmt --check and scripts/build/check-rust-format.mjs passed; the source remains 182 lines; the original CI log provided the red reproduction. Cross-platform impact: file is Unix-only and tests-only changes are under cfg(test).
